Unit tests: 347 passed, 0 failed.
```

1. A written threshold reaches the device: the value read back is the
   value the device holds, a millivolt below the value written.
2. A value outside the range the device can hold is refused, and so is
   one that would cross the thresholds the device holds.
3. A threshold the device does not report is served as NaN and is not
   writable.
4. The alarm follows the threshold that was written: with the sensor
   reading 1.74 V, moving the low warning to 1.779 V asserts it.
5. The three writes that restore the thresholds are one burst, so they
   reach the device as a single request.
6. A write carries the thresholds it does not name as last requested:
   sending back the three thresholds the device reports returns them a
   millivolt lower again, so a threshold nobody wrote would otherwise
   walk down once per write.

[1] https://gerrit.openbmc.org/c/openbmc/dbus-sensors/+/94200/4

